---
source: crates/togi/tests/read/watch.rs
expression: text(&ran.stdout)
---
Show the session dashboard with rootless stock-only day controls

It redraws when the journal changes and on whole-second boundaries for the clock. During an idle trial it holds still, clock included, until the journal changes, a key is pressed or the terminal is resized. togi says in plain words what it is doing and why, how far the current trial has come and what each outcome would lead to. Each CCD shows its cores' offsets beside its night edge U, cap S and applied day offset D; a context panel shows when the night started, its elapsed observed time and each queue's share of it, qualification and reproductions, or the day's hold, step, freeze, firmware, events and vetoes, beside what happened recently. On a terminal, keys switch views: `?` explains the screen, `l` shows the newest 400 journal events, Up/Down and PageUp/PageDown scroll both, Esc returns, and `q` exits the dashboard. A journal scrolled back holds its list still and counts new events until End. It is rendered from the journal, which it reloads only when it changes. On the normal main view, `p` toggles a stock-only pause until cold boot and `f` toggles freeze. These controls use the installed `togi day` command without root; `f` still scrolls help and log.

The screen's words are explained by `?` on the screen itself; the rules behind them are in docs/spec/tuner.md.

On a terminal it fills the screen until interrupted; otherwise it prints one frame of --width by --height and exits. An unreadable or incompatible journal appears in the frame and on stderr, and one-frame watch exits 1; live watch keeps showing the problem and retries when the journal changes. A journal another build wrote, whose first line is not a togi journal header, is refused before any frame: exit 1 with the cutover step on stderr, live or not. A missing journal (no session yet) is not an error. An event of a kind this build does not know is skipped and an unknown field is ignored; the footer says which. The tuning boot shows it on tty1 with `--tuning-boot`, which keeps it open: `q`, `Q`, Ctrl-C and Ctrl-D do nothing in every view and the help and footer leave the quit key out, so only stopping the service, or a signal such as SIGTERM, ends it. A terminal alone never sets it. `NO_COLOR` turns colour off.

Usage: togi watch [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --state-dir <path>
          State directory
          
          [default: /var/lib/togi]

      --width <columns>
          Frame width in columns, 1 to 1000, when stdout is not a terminal
          
          [default: 240]

      --height <rows>
          Frame height in rows, 1 to 1000, when stdout is not a terminal
          
          [default: 67]

      --tuning-boot
          Run as the unattended tuning-boot dashboard: ignore q, Q, Ctrl-C and Ctrl-D in every view and leave the quit key out of the help and footer

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Examples:
  togi watch                                 The session in the default state directory, full screen
  togi --state-dir <dir> watch > frame.txt   One 240x67 frame of the session in <dir>
